Also (openbsd-proton): OpenBSD refuses connect() with EINVAL on a TCP
socket whose previous connect failed, which Windows allows; the socket is
recreated at the same descriptor (sock_recreate_unix_socket()) and the
connect retried.  Stream sockets' fds become cacheable by clients only
once connected, so no client holds the replaced descriptor.

Dual-stack sockets (openbsd-proton): OpenBSD has no IPv4-mapped addresses
on IPv6 sockets.  IPV6_V6ONLY requests for IPv6 sockets come here from
ntdll; turning it off marks an unconnected stream socket dual-stack, and
connect() to ::ffff:a.b.c.d recreates its Unix socket as IPv4 at the same
descriptor and reports the addresses as IPv4-mapped IPv6
(sock_connect_dualstack()).  UDP and listening sockets are not emulated.

